Subject: Pin a for-in source's shape instead of materialising its keys

What this does
  Makes `for..in` iterate a pinned shape rather than a snapshot array of keys
  built when the loop starts.

Why we need it
  The snapshot allocates per loop and duplicates state the source's shape already
  describes.

What it adds
  A pinned shape keeps the source alive and is validated on each step, so a
  mutation that invalidates it abandons the pinned walk and hands the loop to the
  existing implementation, which has already seen every key the pinned walk
  produced. Proxies, arrays with holes and receivers with accessors are not pinned.
  No opcode is added or retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

